00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:21.480
Now back to the show.
(air whooshing)

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:23.900
Almost as long as humanity has existed,

00:02:23.900 --> 00:02:27.020
we've been trying to figure
out how it's all going to end.

00:02:27.020 --> 00:02:29.560
The Vikings, for instance,
told stories about Ragnarok,

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:32.130
the twilight of the gods,
which was a series of events

00:02:32.130 --> 00:02:35.040
that included a great battle,
the death of gods like Odin,

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:37.270
and the earth ultimately being flooded.

00:02:37.270 --> 00:02:38.340
The Aztecs, meanwhile,

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:40.550
not only believed that
the world would end,

00:02:40.550 --> 00:02:42.790
they believe that it already had ended

00:02:42.790 --> 00:02:44.736
on four previous occasions.

00:02:44.736 --> 00:02:48.540
First, it was destroyed by
jaguars, then by a hurricane,

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:51.770
then by a rain of fire,
and then by a great flood.

00:02:51.770 --> 00:02:54.000
This flooding thing is a bit of a theme.

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:56.620
Next, it was going to be
destroyed in an earthquake.

00:02:56.620 --> 00:02:59.540
In fact, for the Aztecs, the
end of the world was an event

00:02:59.540 --> 00:03:03.410
which had to be constantly
warded off by human sacrifices

00:03:03.410 --> 00:03:04.617
to help keep the sun alive.

00:03:04.617 --> 00:03:06.210
But in our opinion,

00:03:06.210 --> 00:03:08.920
no one else has ever really
quite done the end times

00:03:08.920 --> 00:03:10.410
quite like Christianity.

00:03:10.410 --> 00:03:12.490
The story of Revelation
closes out the Bible,

00:03:12.490 --> 00:03:14.300
telling us in no uncertain terms

00:03:14.300 --> 00:03:16.030
that when the end does come,

00:03:16.030 --> 00:03:18.390
it'll make the final
episode of "Game of Thrones"

00:03:18.390 --> 00:03:22.260
look like child's play, including
lots of fire and dragons.

00:03:22.260 --> 00:03:24.860
Of course, at the end, Christ returns

00:03:24.860 --> 00:03:26.620
and redeems everything.
(chiming music)

00:03:26.620 --> 00:03:29.090
And there's no more suffering and death.

00:03:29.090 --> 00:03:31.730
But this brand new start is, of course,

00:03:31.730 --> 00:03:34.350
predicated on a bunch of destruction,

00:03:34.350 --> 00:03:37.860
proving that there's really
no such thing as a free lunch.

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:39.740
Throughout the past couple of centuries,

00:03:39.740 --> 00:03:41.880
religious leaders, fringe
writers, charlatans,

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:45.290
and cultists various stripes
have confidently predicted

00:03:45.290 --> 00:03:46.810
that all the world was gonna end,

00:03:46.810 --> 00:03:50.751
often on some definite date,
and maybe even very soon.

00:03:50.751 --> 00:03:51.980
In the Middle Ages,

00:03:51.980 --> 00:03:54.860
some people got really
fired up about the year 1000

00:03:54.860 --> 00:03:56.840
with the millennium of Christ's birth

00:03:56.840 --> 00:03:59.490
kicking off a wave of apocalyptic mania

00:03:59.490 --> 00:04:03.290
that ended up being pretty anticlimactic.

00:04:03.290 --> 00:04:04.940
Over the course of the next millennium,

00:04:04.940 --> 00:04:07.310
plenty of the apocalyptic-hungry faithful

00:04:07.310 --> 00:04:11.150
would wrongly believe that the
end times had finally come.

00:04:11.150 --> 00:04:12.650
These included the Millerites,

00:04:12.650 --> 00:04:14.040
an American doomsday sect

00:04:14.040 --> 00:04:15.880
from the middle of the 19th century

00:04:15.880 --> 00:04:19.380
who prepared for the apocalypse,
not once, but three times,

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:20.670
before given up

00:04:20.670 --> 00:04:23.340
and deciding they'd have
to commit to being alive.

00:04:23.340 --> 00:04:26.190
All the time, you might
say, was Miller time.

00:04:26.190 --> 00:04:27.023
Members of the group

00:04:27.023 --> 00:04:29.550
later founded the Seventh
Day Adventist church,

00:04:29.550 --> 00:04:32.100
which now claims over 20 million members,

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:34.520
18 million of whom have
knocked on your door.

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:37.360
Meanwhile, over the course
of the 20th century,

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:39.720
the apocalypse got even weirder

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:41.430
with groups like Heaven's Gate preaching

00:04:41.430 --> 00:04:43.630
that the end of the world
will be brought about

00:04:43.630 --> 00:04:47.460
through UFOs, which is
definitely more creative

00:04:47.460 --> 00:04:49.945
than the terrifyingly
plausible possibility

00:04:49.945 --> 00:04:51.950
that the earth might be destroyed

00:04:51.950 --> 00:04:53.970
through manmade climate change.

00:04:53.970 --> 00:04:56.630
But whether or not
you're in an actual cult,

00:04:56.630 --> 00:04:59.110
the sense that we're all about to die

00:04:59.110 --> 00:05:01.320
has steadily become more universal.

00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:03.870
But why people always been so obsessed

00:05:03.870 --> 00:05:05.240
with the end of the world?

00:05:05.240 --> 00:05:07.700
One general fact about
apocalypse narratives

00:05:07.700 --> 00:05:11.450
is that even though the end of
humankind would be very bad,

00:05:11.450 --> 00:05:15.120
the apocalypse brings about
an ultimately positive result.

00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:17.870
At the end of the Ragnarok
story, two human survivors

00:05:17.870 --> 00:05:20.450
are supposed to repopulate
the earth after the flood.

00:05:20.450 --> 00:05:23.920
After Christ's second coming,
death no longer exists.

00:05:23.920 --> 00:05:25.230
After the UFOs come,

00:05:25.230 --> 00:05:27.780
the cult members will
transcend their physical bodies

00:05:27.780 --> 00:05:30.710
and realize some sort of
paradise in outer space.

00:05:30.710 --> 00:05:33.200
Plus, matching sneakers and tracksuits.

00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:35.150
Interestingly, one interpretation

00:05:35.150 --> 00:05:36.320
of the Revelation narrative

00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:38.330
is that it was actually a story told

00:05:38.330 --> 00:05:41.180
by persecuted members of
the early Christian Church

00:05:41.180 --> 00:05:42.400
to comfort themselves.

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:44.600
One day, the old world would fall,

00:05:44.600 --> 00:05:47.860
and the emperor would stop
feeding you to lions for sport.

00:05:47.860 --> 00:05:49.090
The end of the world is, therefore,

00:05:49.090 --> 00:05:51.240
supposed to redeem humankind

00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:54.930
or at least the people who did
or believed the right thing.

00:05:54.930 --> 00:05:56.720
And if the world around you sucks,

00:05:56.720 --> 00:05:58.560
it's very comforting to hope

00:05:58.560 --> 00:06:01.030
that a fresh start is
right around the corner.

00:06:01.030 --> 00:06:02.310
This is especially the case

00:06:02.310 --> 00:06:03.970
for historically oppressed groups

00:06:03.970 --> 00:06:06.020
who were waiting for some kind of messiah

00:06:06.020 --> 00:06:07.760
to destroy this (beep) world

00:06:07.760 --> 00:06:10.910
and create a new one grounded
in some type of justice.

00:06:10.910 --> 00:06:12.280
Psychoanalysts like Freud

00:06:12.280 --> 00:06:14.040
have offered their own interpretations

00:06:14.040 --> 00:06:15.360
on this religious narrative,

00:06:15.360 --> 00:06:17.630
using the concept the death drive

00:06:17.630 --> 00:06:20.400
to explain the fundamental human instinct

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:23.150
to revert back towards an inorganic state.

00:06:23.150 --> 00:06:26.060
And in his work, "Civilization
And Its Discontents,"

00:06:26.060 --> 00:06:27.780
Freud argues that the reason

00:06:27.780 --> 00:06:29.980
we have a civilization in the first place

00:06:29.980 --> 00:06:32.640
is to contain fundamental
human aggressions

00:06:32.640 --> 00:06:34.450
caused by this death drive.

00:06:34.450 --> 00:06:36.700
On this reading, the
desire for the end of life

00:06:36.700 --> 00:06:39.235
is just sort of wired
into our unconscious.

00:06:39.235 --> 00:06:41.070
One good boy who read Freud

00:06:41.070 --> 00:06:44.540
was Frankfurt School critical
theorist Theodor Adorno.

00:06:44.540 --> 00:06:46.720
Adorno lived through
the rise of the Nazis,

00:06:46.720 --> 00:06:50.330
escaped the Holocaust by fleeing
to America, which he hated,

00:06:50.330 --> 00:06:52.780
and witnessed the
invention of the atom bomb.

00:06:52.780 --> 00:06:54.650
For him, all of this was a sign

00:06:54.650 --> 00:06:57.060
that life was so irredeemably bad

00:06:57.060 --> 00:06:59.030
that he named it the wrong life.

00:06:59.030 --> 00:07:02.560
According to Adorno, right
living was no longer possible.

00:07:02.560 --> 00:07:05.680
For him, this extended
from seriously awful things

00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:08.730
like death camps to
pleasant things like jazz,

00:07:08.730 --> 00:07:10.360
all the way down to banal things

00:07:10.360 --> 00:07:12.340
like door handles and refrigerators.

00:07:12.340 --> 00:07:14.430
No, seriously, he wrote about this stuff.

00:07:14.430 --> 00:07:16.647
Adorno explored these
thoughts in his book,

00:07:16.647 --> 00:07:20.010
"Minima Moralia," a sort
of philosophical document

00:07:20.010 --> 00:07:22.470
of his experiences in exile in America.

00:07:22.470 --> 00:07:24.410
To understand "Minima Moralia,"

00:07:24.410 --> 00:07:27.050
imagine a book written by Larry David

00:07:27.050 --> 00:07:28.930
if he just read loads of Hegel

00:07:28.930 --> 00:07:31.940
instead of writing about Soup
Nazis and no-fap contests.

00:07:31.940 --> 00:07:35.780
After approximately 200 or so
pages detailing the many ways

00:07:35.780 --> 00:07:38.990
in which modern life both sucks
and is completely hopeless,

00:07:38.990 --> 00:07:43.110
Adorno declares that actually
we can do one good thing.

00:07:43.110 --> 00:07:46.160
In the book's final section,
apply titled "Finale,"

00:07:46.160 --> 00:07:48.450
he writes that, "The only philosophy

00:07:48.450 --> 00:07:51.670
which can be responsibly
practiced in the face of despair

00:07:51.670 --> 00:07:54.100
is the attempt to contemplate all things

00:07:54.100 --> 00:07:55.550
as they would present themselves

00:07:55.550 --> 00:07:57.410
from the standpoint of redemption.

00:07:57.410 --> 00:07:59.927
We have to develop
perspectives," Adorno said,

00:07:59.927 --> 00:08:03.440
"that displace and estrange
the world, reveal it to be,

00:08:03.440 --> 00:08:06.900
with its rifts and crevices,
as indigent and distorted

00:08:06.900 --> 00:08:09.840
as it will appear one day
in the messianic light."

00:08:09.840 --> 00:08:12.050
So in short, if everything is terrible,

00:08:12.050 --> 00:08:15.220
if all life is wrong, and
we can't live it rightly,

00:08:15.220 --> 00:08:17.720
there's still one thing we can do:

00:08:17.720 --> 00:08:19.430
look forward to the rapture.
(angelic music)

00:08:19.430 --> 00:08:22.175
Now Adorno's messianism
was merely theoretical.

00:08:22.175 --> 00:08:24.920
He just thought people should
try to think about the world

00:08:24.920 --> 00:08:27.360
as if the messiah was about to come down

00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:29.280
and finally set everything right,

00:08:29.280 --> 00:08:31.910
but it's certainly possible to
see how this sort of thinking

00:08:31.910 --> 00:08:35.460
might translate into a yearning
for the literal apocalypse.

00:08:35.460 --> 00:08:38.020
An alternative to Adorno's
messianism can be found

00:08:38.020 --> 00:08:40.620
in the work of German
philosopher, Walter Benjamin.

00:08:40.620 --> 00:08:44.250
For Benjamin, an apocalyptic
hell wasn't in the near future.

00:08:44.250 --> 00:08:45.660
It was already here

00:08:45.660 --> 00:08:47.940
for the majority of the
world's suppressed people,

00:08:47.940 --> 00:08:51.410
but unlike Adorno, he thought
revolutionary politics

00:08:51.410 --> 00:08:54.040
was all about bringing
about a messianic moment

00:08:54.040 --> 00:08:56.120
in which time would rupture.

00:08:56.120 --> 00:08:58.250
This would mean the end of the old world

00:08:58.250 --> 00:09:00.170
and the birth of something new.

00:09:00.170 --> 00:09:02.510
In this way, theological redemption

00:09:02.510 --> 00:09:04.550
becomes political redemption.

00:09:04.550 --> 00:09:07.190
Of course, this argument
might sound slightly odd

00:09:07.190 --> 00:09:10.490
when applied to climate
change or nuclear war.

00:09:10.490 --> 00:09:13.860
After all, how are those
potentially apocalyptic events

00:09:13.860 --> 00:09:15.310
supposed to be redeeming?

00:09:15.310 --> 00:09:19.240
What sort of messianism is to
be found in the world burning?

00:09:19.240 --> 00:09:20.990
And that's not surprising.

00:09:20.990 --> 00:09:22.870
Humans tend to like being right

00:09:22.870 --> 00:09:25.870
to the point where when
something huge is on the line,

00:09:25.870 --> 00:09:29.160
it easily devolves into moral absolutism,

00:09:29.160 --> 00:09:31.500
like say the end of the world,

00:09:31.500 --> 00:09:34.410
or, you know, whether
pineapple belongs on pizza.

00:09:34.410 --> 00:09:37.260
The doomer mindset makes
its own sort of sense.

00:09:37.260 --> 00:09:40.360
The doomer has abandoned
any possibility of hope,

00:09:40.360 --> 00:09:42.060
and they may find that comforting.

00:09:42.060 --> 00:09:43.370
Maybe the world will end.

00:09:43.370 --> 00:09:46.660
Maybe the world will end
soon, but at least if it does,

00:09:46.660 --> 00:09:48.660
the doomer won't have been wrong.

00:09:48.660 --> 00:09:51.090
In their minds, the optimistic people,

00:09:51.090 --> 00:09:53.180
those who thought that
maybe the end of the world

00:09:53.180 --> 00:09:54.380
might have been prevented,

00:09:54.380 --> 00:09:57.230
will not only have to endure
the end of all things,

00:09:57.230 --> 00:09:59.400
they'll also have been very wrong.

00:09:59.400 --> 00:10:01.750
As the flaming commits fall to Earth,

00:10:01.750 --> 00:10:04.580
they'll know they've
been played for chumps.

00:10:04.580 --> 00:10:08.280
But this idea that the world
is already definitely ending,

00:10:08.280 --> 00:10:10.509
an idea shared by our friend, Bo Burnham.

00:10:10.509 --> 00:10:12.783
♪ You say the whole world's ending ♪

00:10:12.783 --> 00:10:14.830
♪ And the end is already there ♪

00:10:14.830 --> 00:10:16.170
As explored by philosophers

00:10:16.170 --> 00:10:19.110
and anthropologist writing
about the Anthropocene,

00:10:19.110 --> 00:10:21.420
i.e., the era of earth
that has been marked

00:10:21.420 --> 00:10:24.460
by humankind domination
of geology and ecology.

00:10:24.460 --> 00:10:27.630
Many of these theorists think
the age of the Anthropocene,

00:10:27.630 --> 00:10:31.000
and thus humanity, is
absolutely coming to an end

00:10:31.000 --> 00:10:33.150
and that it's all our fault.

00:10:33.150 --> 00:10:36.140
Scholar, Gregory Marks, in an
essay on the end of the world,

00:10:36.140 --> 00:10:39.210
quotes Deborah Danowski and
Eduardo Viveiros de Castro

00:10:39.210 --> 00:10:40.710
has saying that, "It is certain,

00:10:40.710 --> 00:10:43.980
that although it began with
us, it will end without us:

00:10:43.980 --> 00:10:46.330
The Anthropocene will only give way

00:10:46.330 --> 00:10:48.104
to a new geological epoch

00:10:48.104 --> 00:10:51.400
long after we have disappeared
from the face of the Earth."

00:10:51.400 --> 00:10:53.220
So if the end of the world ever comes,

00:10:53.220 --> 00:10:56.117
it will almost certainly be our fault.

00:10:56.117 --> 00:10:58.870
But that that's weirdly good news.

00:10:58.870 --> 00:11:01.020
Because even if you don't
have a lot of power,

00:11:01.020 --> 00:11:02.580
you do have some.

00:11:02.580 --> 00:11:04.800
It might not be obvious
what we ought to do,

00:11:04.800 --> 00:11:07.680
but we do have at least
some control over our lives,

00:11:07.680 --> 00:11:09.270
and thus in some small way,

00:11:09.270 --> 00:11:11.750
over the fate of the species as a whole.

00:11:11.750 --> 00:11:13.867
This is what Jean-Paul Sartre
meant when he said that,

00:11:13.867 --> 00:11:16.130
"Existence precedes essence."

00:11:16.130 --> 00:11:19.070
While we might find ourselves
tricked in many ways,

00:11:19.070 --> 00:11:20.920
there is something about humanity,

00:11:20.920 --> 00:11:23.220
which makes us free to define ourselves

00:11:23.220 --> 00:11:25.050
in relation to those restrictions,

00:11:25.050 --> 00:11:27.290
to spot possibilities for action

00:11:27.290 --> 00:11:30.620
and choose to actualize
them whenever we can.

00:11:30.620 --> 00:11:33.070
And what happens when we
don't embrace that freedom,

00:11:33.070 --> 00:11:35.020
when we use external circumstances

00:11:35.020 --> 00:11:37.150
as an excuse for our inaction?

00:11:37.150 --> 00:11:39.250
That's what Sartre called bad faith.

00:11:39.250 --> 00:11:43.340
For him, our freedom means
that we are without excuse.

00:11:43.340 --> 00:11:45.890
We are responsible for our own actions,

00:11:45.890 --> 00:11:47.230
for the good or bad we do

00:11:47.230 --> 00:11:50.180
within whatever conditions
we happen to find ourselves.

00:11:50.180 --> 00:11:51.800
Doomerism, on the other hand,

00:11:51.800 --> 00:11:54.690
seeks to give everyone
the ultimate excuse.

00:11:54.690 --> 00:11:56.313
There's no point doing anything

00:11:56.313 --> 00:11:59.550
since we're all going to die soon anyway.

00:11:59.550 --> 00:12:02.030
That said, even Sartre
shifted his position

00:12:02.030 --> 00:12:04.210
in his later work where he tried to fuse

00:12:04.210 --> 00:12:06.810
the existentialist
emphasis on human freedom

00:12:06.810 --> 00:12:09.430
with a Marxist emphasis
on social inequality.

00:12:09.430 --> 00:12:12.630
He eventually argued that even
our freedom can be limited

00:12:12.630 --> 00:12:14.170
by material conditions

00:12:14.170 --> 00:12:15.630
and that it is actually the work

00:12:15.630 --> 00:12:17.370
of collective human projects

00:12:17.370 --> 00:12:19.770
to affect any real change in the world.

00:12:19.770 --> 00:12:21.820
For Sartre, the looming end of the world

00:12:21.820 --> 00:12:23.590
would be just the type of threat

00:12:23.590 --> 00:12:26.100
that could unify disparate humans together

00:12:26.100 --> 00:12:28.930
into what he would call a group-in-fusion.

00:12:28.930 --> 00:12:32.150
People who previously had nothing
in common could be rallied

00:12:32.150 --> 00:12:35.070
and united in the mission
of stopping the apocalypse.

00:12:35.070 --> 00:12:37.520
For him, this totalizing doom

00:12:37.520 --> 00:12:39.610
might be just the sort of thing

00:12:39.610 --> 00:12:43.470
that could lead to an uprising
of real collective freedom.

00:12:43.470 --> 00:12:46.040
So it'd be like if there
was, let's say, I don't know,

00:12:46.040 --> 00:12:48.780
a looming ecological crisis on the horizon

00:12:48.780 --> 00:12:51.290
that was certain to radically
alter all human life,

00:12:51.290 --> 00:12:52.980
and those humans could get together

00:12:52.980 --> 00:12:54.530
and use their elective power

00:12:54.530 --> 00:12:57.420
to stop that thing from
happening or something like that.

00:12:57.420 --> 00:12:58.253
Does that sound crazy?

00:12:58.253 --> 00:13:00.700
Is that a crazy example,
that if the world is ending,

00:13:00.700 --> 00:13:02.160
we get together, and we try to fix it?

00:13:02.160 --> 00:13:03.430
Is that stupid? Am I crazy?

00:13:03.430 --> 00:13:05.503
Are you crazy, is anyone? I don't know.

00:13:06.520 --> 00:13:08.190
So what do you all think?

00:13:08.190 --> 00:13:10.670
Would the apocalypse really
be the ultimate excuse

00:13:10.670 --> 00:13:13.110
to never pay back our student loans?

00:13:13.110 --> 00:13:14.780
Do we all really secretly think

00:13:14.780 --> 00:13:16.330
that the end times are coming?

00:13:16.330 --> 00:13:19.530
And if so, what does that train
of thought do to a culture?

00:13:19.530 --> 00:13:21.320
Let us know in the comments.

00:13:21.320 --> 00:13:23.740
Hit that subscribe button
like it's the flip top

00:13:23.740 --> 00:13:25.890
on a can of beans in your fallout shelter.

00:13:25.890 --> 00:13:27.982
And don't forget to ring that bell.

00:13:27.982 --> 00:13:30.450
Thanks always to our patrons
